# Architecture

## Status

Early development. This document is the source of truth for architecture and
conventions in {{ project_name }}. Update it as durable decisions are made, and
capture the reasoning in a feature-scoped ADR.

## Architecture Principles

- Documents are the source of truth for intent and constraints.
- Code changes are generated from documented feature requirements.
- Decisions with lasting architectural impact belong in ADRs.
- Prefer the smallest useful change and explicit contracts over broad restructuring.
- Keep concerns separate and changes reviewable.

## System Overview

{% if project_type == 'full_project' %}
{{ project_name }} is a Django project built on:

- Python {{ python_version }}, Django {{ django_version }}{% if django_version == '5.2' %} LTS{% endif %}, PostgreSQL {{ postgres_version }}.
- A custom user model (`apps/accounts`), identified by
  {{ 'email address' if user_identifier == 'email' else 'username' }} (`AUTH_USER_MODEL`).
- A home page at `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/` (view, URL, `templates/`).
{% if use_shinobi %}
- A django-shinobi (Django Ninja) API layer with JWT auth under `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/`.
{% else %}
- The Django admin site (no HTTP API layer in this project).
{% endif %}
{% if use_allauth %}
- Session-based login/signup/logout via django-allauth (`/accounts/...`){% if use_shinobi %}, entirely
  separate from the JWT API auth above — allauth handles browser sessions, the API
  issues its own tokens{% endif %}.
{% endif %}
- Background work via `django-tasks`{% if use_redis %}, running on the RQ/Redis backend with a dedicated worker{% else %} (synchronous in-process backend){% endif %}.
- Email via SMTP, caught in dev by a `mailpit` service (web UI at `localhost:8025`)
  and captured in-memory during tests (`locmem` backend); production sends via
  {{ {"ses": "Amazon SES", "postmark": "Postmark", "mailgun": "Mailgun", "sendgrid": "SendGrid"}[email_provider] }}
  through `django-anymail`.
- Media file storage:
  {{ {"local": "local filesystem (Django's built-in `FileSystemStorage`, no extra infra needed)", "s3": "Amazon S3 via `django-storages`", "azure": "Azure Blob Storage via `django-storages`"}[storage_backend] }}.
- Structured JSON logging via `structlog` (stdout only — no file handlers in
  the container, so a log collector reads it from there). A `request_id` is
  bound to every log line for the life of a request
  (`config.logging.RequestContextMiddleware`, honors an incoming
  `X-Request-ID` header), and can be passed along explicitly to a
  `django-tasks` job so its logs correlate back to the request that enqueued
  it (see the example task in `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/tasks.py`).
{% if use_caddy %}
- Caddy reverse-proxies `{{ domain_name }}` to `web`, terminating TLS with
  automatic HTTPS (Let's Encrypt). Its access logs are JSON on stdout, tagged
  with the same request ID the app's structlog middleware uses, so proxy and
  app logs for one request line up. `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` in `prod.py`
  tells Django the original request was HTTPS even though Caddy talks to
  gunicorn/uvicorn over plain HTTP internally — without it,
  `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT` would redirect-loop. Not meant to provide real TLS in
  local dev (no public DNS/inbound ports there); `docker-compose.override.yml`
  publishes `web`'s port directly instead.
  Caddy lives in its own `docker-compose.caddy.yml`, started independently
  of `docker-compose.yml` — `docker network create caddy_net` once per host,
  then `docker compose -f docker-compose.caddy.yml up -d` any time. This
  lets one Caddy instance front multiple projects on the same host sharing
  `caddy_net`, the same way one Vector instance can serve multiple projects
  (see below) — if you deploy more than one project from this template on a
  single host, only start `docker-compose.caddy.yml` from *one* of them;
  the others just need `use_caddy=true` for the `web`-side wiring
  (`container_name`, joining `caddy_net`) and their own site added to
  whichever Caddy instance is already running. `web`'s container name is
  fixed (`{{ project_slug }}-web`, matching the Caddyfile's `reverse_proxy`
  target) rather than the bare service name `web`, since that would collide
  with every other project's `web` service on the same shared network.
{% endif %}
{% if use_log_shipping %}
- Vector ships every container's logs to a centralized VictoriaLogs instance,
  tagging each log line with `host`/`project`/`service`/`env`{% if use_caddy %} and reshaping
  Caddy's access-log fields{% endif %}. Configured via `VICTORIALOGS_ENDPOINT`/`_USER`/
  `_PASSWORD` env vars rather than at generation time, since a centralized
  log store is typically shared across more than one project. A disk buffer
  lets logs survive a network or log-host outage. Example LogsQL queries:
  `request_id:"<id>"` (everything for one request, across every service),
  `_time:1h level:ERROR` (recent errors, every service).
  Run only one Vector instance per Docker host, even with multiple projects
  generated from this template on it — Vector reads the whole host's Docker
  socket, so one instance per project would ship the same logs redundantly.
  Vector lives in its own `docker-compose.vector.yml`, started independently
  of `docker-compose.yml` (`docker compose -f docker-compose.vector.yml up
  -d`) with its own Compose project name, so `docker compose up
  --remove-orphans` on the application stack can't remove it. It needs no
  shared network to do this (unlike a reverse proxy would): it talks to the
  Docker socket directly, not to any container by name.
  Also runs under Podman, verified directly (Vector's `docker_logs` source
  works unchanged against Podman's Docker-compatible API socket) — but
  Podman's rootless socket isn't at `/var/run/docker.sock` by default, so
  set `DOCKER_SOCK` in `.env` to wherever `podman info` shows it (usually
  `/run/user/<uid>/podman/podman.sock`), after enabling it with `systemctl
  --user enable --now podman.socket`. Leave `DOCKER_SOCK` unset on Docker.
{% if use_victorialogs %}
  A `docker-compose.victorialogs.yml` for the log store itself is also
  generated (via the separate `use_victorialogs` toggle — most projects
  only ship logs, so don't need this; it's for whichever one project
  stands up the shared log host), standalone and with its own Compose
  project name for the same orphan-cleanup reason as the other stacks, but
  meant to be started on exactly one dedicated log host for your whole
  fleet, not on every machine running this project. It binds to
  `127.0.0.1` only, since VictoriaLogs has no authentication of its own; if
  other hosts need to reach it, put a reverse proxy with TLS and auth in
  front (Caddy, the same way `docker-compose.caddy.yml` fronts this
  project's own `web`).
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if use_error_tracking %}
- Error tracking via the standard `sentry-sdk`, using `DjangoIntegration`{% if use_redis %}
  and `RqIntegration` (so RQ task failures are captured too){% endif %}.
  `ERROR_TRACKING_DSN` works against Sentry itself or any
  Sentry-protocol-compatible self-hosted alternative (e.g. BugSink,
  GlitchTip) — just a DSN, no code changes either way. Only initialized in
  `prod.py`, not dev/test, to avoid reporting local noise. Events are
  tagged with the same `request_id` the app's structlog middleware uses and
  the signed-in user's id (`config/logging.py`,
  `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/auth.py`), so an error-tracking event and its
  correlated log lines are both findable from the same id.
{% endif %}
{% if use_vite %}
- A Pico.css + Vite frontend build pipeline (django-vite) for the home page.
{% endif %}
- Tooling: uv, ruff, pyright, pytest, and Docker Compose.
{% if django_version == '6.1' %}

**Known Django 6.1 caveats (not yet addressed, tracked for future work):**

- Django 6.0+ ships a built-in `django.tasks` background-task framework using
  the same `TASKS` setting name as the third-party `django-tasks`/
  `django-tasks-rq` packages this project uses (see `config/settings/base.py`).
  They don't currently conflict — this project's own `TASKS` backend classes
  still work — but migrating to Django's native framework hasn't been
  evaluated.
- The `EMAIL_HOST`/`EMAIL_HOST_USER`/`EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD`/`EMAIL_BACKEND`/
  `EMAIL_PORT`/`EMAIL_USE_TLS` settings this project configures in
  `config/settings/base.py` are deprecated as of Django 6.1
  (`RemovedInDjango70Warning`) in favor of a new `MAILERS` setting; they still
  work today but this project hasn't been migrated to `MAILERS`.
{% endif %}
